#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"%d of %d studies have a cell of 0 or 1. The bivariate model used here "
"approximates the logit sensitivity and specificity as normal, which is "
"biased when cells are that small, and any zero cell additionally needs a "
"continuity correction. In simulations of high-accuracy tests with cells this "
"small, the nominal 95%% confidence interval for the pooled value on the "
"sparse margin covered the true value in only about 70%% of meta-analyses, or "
"fewer, and the pooled value was biased toward lower accuracy. Treat the "
"pooled pair and its interval as approximate, and confirm them in software "
"offering a binomial bivariate (generalised linear mixed) model before "
"relying on them."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"%d of %d studies have a cell smaller than 5. The bivariate model used here "
"approximates the logit sensitivity and specificity as normal, and with cells "
"this small the studies with the fewest errors get the least weight. In "
"simulations of high-accuracy tests with 2 to 8 false negatives per study, "
"the nominal 95%% confidence interval for the pooled sensitivity covered the "
"true value in only about 80%% of meta-analyses, and the pooled value was "
"slightly too low. Read the interval as too narrow, and confirm the pooled "
"pair in software offering a binomial bivariate (generalised linear mixed) "
"model before relying on it."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"%d of %d studies have fractional counts. Cell counts are numbers of "
"participants; check whether percentages or rates were entered. These studies "
"are excluded."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"%d of %d studies have no false negatives. Each of them enters the model only "
"after a continuity correction, so the pooled sensitivity, its confidence "
"interval, its between-study variance, LR- and the diagnostic odds ratio are "
"set largely by the size of that correction rather than by the data: a "
"smaller correction gives a higher sensitivity and a much smaller LR-. The "
"interval is not a data-derived precision. If all studies shared one "
"sensitivity, the raw counts (%s false negatives among %s diseased "
"participants) would give an exact %s%% confidence interval starting at "
"%.2f%%, and LR- would have no lower limit above zero the data can set. "
"Report the raw counts and that bound alongside the pooled value, and do not "
"read the LR- grading as established."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"%d of %d studies have no false positives. Each of them enters the model only "
"after a continuity correction, so the pooled specificity, its confidence "
"interval, its between-study variance, LR+ and the diagnostic odds ratio are "
"set largely by the size of that correction rather than by the data: a "
"smaller correction gives a higher specificity and a much larger LR+. The "
"interval is not a data-derived precision. If all studies shared one "
"specificity, the raw counts (%s false positives among %s non-diseased "
"participants) would give an exact %s%% confidence interval starting at "
"%.2f%%, and LR+ would have no upper limit the data can set. Report the raw "
"counts and that bound alongside the pooled value, and do not read the LR+ "
"grading as established."
msgstr ""

