#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"%d study/studies contain a zero cell. A continuity correction of 0.5 was "
"applied to those studies only so the model could be fitted. Continuity "
"corrections shift the pooled estimate. The option '+0.5 to all cells of "
"zero-cell studies' fits the same model as this default, so to see how "
"sensitive your result is, compare with '+0.5 to the zero cells only'."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"%d study/studies contain a zero cell. No correction is applied to the data: "
"every model (bivariate, proportional-hazards SROC, heterogeneity and "
"meta-regression) adds 0.5 to all four cells of those studies, so each model "
"uses every study. Deeks' test and the funnel plot use the raw counts and add "
"0.5 to every study."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"%d study/studies have no identifier; they are labeled 'Unnamed study' in the "
"tables and the forest plot."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"%d study/studies with a zero cell were corrected by adding 1/N to every "
"cell, where N is the study size. A cell of 1/N gives such a study a logit "
"variance of about N, so the models give it almost no weight, and these are "
"the studies with 100%% sensitivity or specificity: the pooled accuracy is "
"pulled toward lower values. In simulations of high-accuracy tests the 95%% "
"confidence interval for pooled sensitivity covered the true value in only "
"31-55%% of meta-analyses under this option, against 58-69%% under the "
"default. This is not a published correction; use the default unless you are "
"deliberately running a sensitivity analysis."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
