"<br><br><strong>Your positive LR of %.2f</strong> provides <em>moderate</em> "
"evidence FOR disease when test is positive."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<br><br><strong>Your positive LR of %.2f</strong> provides <em>strong</em> "
"evidence FOR disease when test is positive."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<br><br><strong>Your positive LR of %.2f</strong> provides <em>weak</em> "
"evidence FOR disease when test is positive."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<em>Note:</em> These are simple averages, not meta-analytic pooled estimates."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<em>Note:</em> Zero-cell corrections can introduce bias, especially in large "
"studies. Results should be interpreted with caution."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<em>The bands below are Higgins' conventional cut-points from intervention "
"meta-analysis. They are not established thresholds for diagnostic accuracy, "
"where a high I[[SUP2]] commonly reflects studies using different positivity "
"thresholds - the situation the SROC curve exists to model - rather than a "
"reason to abandon the analysis. Treat them as rough orientation, and prefer "
"the prediction region.</em>"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<em>These features do not establish that publication bias is absent: "
"asymmetry tests have low power, and are unreliable with fewer than 10 "
"studies.</em>"
msgstr ""

#: R/swimmerplot.b.R
msgid "<p><strong>Error:</strong> {message}</p>"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong> Clinical Insight:</strong> The plotted SROC curve is derived from "
"the <em>bivariate model</em>. The proportional-hazards SROC table is a "
"separate compact model of diagnostic accuracy and between-study variation; "
"it should not be interpreted as a Rutter-Gatsonis HSROC threshold/accuracy "
"table."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong> Pro Tip:</strong> Always interpret meta-analysis results in the "
"context of your specific clinical population and intended use. A test "
"excellent for one application may be inappropriate for another."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong> Recommendation:</strong> Start with REML unless you have specific "
"methodological requirements. It provides the best balance of statistical "
"properties and computational stability for diagnostic test meta-analysis."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong> Tip:</strong> Start with the bivariate model and forest plot to "
"understand overall performance, then explore heterogeneity sources with "
"meta-regression if needed."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong> When the Funnel Plot is Asymmetric:</strong>"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Above Diagonal:</strong> Better than chance performance"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Action Needed:</strong> If the funnel is asymmetric, look for a "
"clinical explanation first, then for unpublished studies"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Analysis Status:</strong> Pooled estimates are not available; the "
"figures below are descriptive."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Analysis Type:</strong> Diagnostic test accuracy meta-analysis of %d "
"studies"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Asymmetric Funnel:</strong> Studies concentrated on one side at the "
"imprecise end of the plot"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Balanced Performance:</strong> Consider clinical costs of false "
"positives vs false negatives"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Before running analysis, verify:</strong>"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Bivariate modeling</strong> - Jointly analyzes sensitivity and "
"specificity"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Bivariate Random-Effects Model (Recommended Primary "
"Approach):</strong>"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Bivariate Random-Effects Model (Recommended):</strong> Jointly "
"analyzes sensitivity and specificity accounting for correlation"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Choose the appropriate estimation method for your "
"meta-analysis:</strong>"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Clinical Implications:</strong> Population-specific predictive values"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Confidence region (dashed):</strong> how precisely the POOLED point "
"is estimated. It shrinks as more studies are added."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Confirmatory Testing:</strong> Prioritize specificity; the level "
"needed depends on prevalence and on the cost of a false positive, not on a "
"fixed band"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Consistent Performance:</strong> low measured heterogeneity means "
"the included studies produced similar estimates; with few studies it is "
"imprecisely estimated and does not by itself show that performance transfers "
"to a new setting"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Consistent Results:</strong> Points clustered together = low "
"heterogeneity"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Deeks' Test p < 0.05:</strong> Statistical evidence of funnel plot "
"asymmetry - evidence of asymmetry, not of publication bias; see the causes "
"listed below"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Deeks' Test p ≥ 0.05:</strong> No statistical evidence of asymmetry"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Diagonal Line:</strong> Represents random chance (no discriminative "
"ability)"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>DOR &lt; 5:</strong> Limited discriminative ability"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>DOR > 25:</strong> Excellent overall discriminative ability"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>DOR 10-25:</strong> Good discriminative ability"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>DOR 5-10:</strong> Moderate discriminative ability"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Each Point:</strong> One study in your meta-analysis"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Essential Variables (Required):</strong>"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Expected Pattern:</strong> Inverted funnel shape if no bias present"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>False negatives (FN):</strong> Number incorrectly identified as "
"negative"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>False positives (FP):</strong> Number incorrectly identified as "
"positive"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Fixed Effects:</strong> Assumes every study shares the exact same "
"sensitivity and specificity (τ² = 0). Under this assumption, the SROC curve "
"and prediction region are undefined, and confidence intervals are narrower "
"by construction."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Fixed Effects:</strong> Use when between-study heterogeneity is "
"minimal or you want to assume all studies estimate the same underlying "
"effect size."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Forest Plot:</strong> Displays individual study results for "
"sensitivity and specificity with confidence intervals. Each study is "
"represented by a point (estimate) with horizontal lines (confidence "
"intervals)."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Funnel Plot:</strong> Assesses publication bias by plotting study "
"precision against effect size (log diagnostic odds ratio)."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Gap in Lower Region:</strong> Small studies with low accuracy absent"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Geographic region:</strong> Study location for population analysis"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Geographic/Temporal:</strong> Population differences, technology "
"evolution"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Heterogeneity assessment</strong> - Evaluates consistency across "
"studies"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Heterogeneity:</strong> Clinical differences can mimic publication "
"bias"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Heterogeneity:</strong> I[[SUP2]] values and potential sources "
"investigated"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Holling Proportional-Hazards SROC Model:</strong>"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Horizontal Lines:</strong> Wilson score confidence intervals (at the "
"chosen confidence level) showing precision of estimates"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>I[[SUP2]] &gt; 75%:</strong> Considerable heterogeneity - a single "
"pooled point is unlikely to describe the evidence; investigate thresholds, "
"spectrum and reference standards, and report the SROC curve and prediction "
"region"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>I[[SUP2]] < 25%:</strong> Little heterogeneity detected on this "
"margin. I[[SUP2]] is imprecisely estimated when few studies are pooled and "
"does not describe the bivariate model, so on its own it does not establish "
"that pooling is appropriate - inspect the prediction region and check that "
"positivity thresholds, patient spectrum and reference standards are "
"comparable"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>I[[SUP2]] 25-50%:</strong> Moderate heterogeneity - investigate "
"potential sources"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>I[[SUP2]] 50-75%:</strong> Substantial heterogeneity - pooling "
"questionable"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Important:</strong> Sensitivity and specificity are test "
"characteristics, but clinicians need predictive values that depend on "
"disease prevalence in their population. See the <em>Predictive Values by "
"Prevalence</em> table below for estimated PPV and NPV across typical "
"prevalence settings based on the pooled accuracy of this meta-analysis."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Individual Studies:</strong> Circles sized by sample size"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Interpretation:</strong> a negative result reduces, but does not "
"remove, the possibility of disease at this sensitivity."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Interpretation:</strong> at this sensitivity a substantial "
"proportion of diseased patients are expected to test negative."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Interpretation:</strong> at this specificity a large share of "
"positive results are expected to be false positives."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Interpretation:</strong> at this specificity an appreciable share of "
"positive results are expected to be false positives."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Left Panel (Sensitivity):</strong> Proportion of diseased cases "
"correctly identified"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Likelihood Ratios:</strong> For clinical decision-making context"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Limitations:</strong> Study quality, missing data, generalizability"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Maximum Likelihood:</strong> Alternative estimation approach when "
"maximum likelihood estimation is specifically preferred or required by study "
"protocol."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Meta-Regression:</strong> Investigates sources of heterogeneity "
"using study-level covariates"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Method of Moments:</strong> Classical moment-based estimation "
"method, useful for comparison with older meta-analyses or when computational "
"resources are limited."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Method:</strong> %s"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Methodological:</strong> Reference standards, blinding, cut-off "
"thresholds"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Narrow CIs:</strong> Large studies with precise estimates"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Negative Likelihood Ratio:</strong> %.2f - A negative test is %.1fx "
"more likely in healthy than disease"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Negative Likelihood Ratio:</strong> %.2f - Interpretation unstable "
"(sensitivity ≈ 100%%)."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Negative Likelihood Ratio:</strong> Not estimable with the current "
"data. LR- is (1 - sensitivity) / specificity, so it needs a pooled "
"specificity above zero and finite pooled estimates; here the pooled "
"specificity is 0 or an estimate did not converge. Read the pooled "
"specificity and its confidence interval in the summary above to see which."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Not external validation:</strong> a meta-analysis summarizes "
"existing published evidence and inherits any publication, spectrum or "
"reference-standard bias of the included studies; it is not a prospective "
"evaluation in an intended-use population with a pre-specified threshold"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Note:</strong> its confidence interval (%.2f-%.2f) spans more than "
"one evidence category, so this grading is not firmly established by the "
"pooled data."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Note:</strong> the confidence interval spans more than one "
"performance category, so this classification is not firmly established by "
"the pooled data."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Number of Studies:</strong> %d"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Optional Variables for Meta-Regression:</strong>"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Other Causes:</strong> Different thresholds, narrower patient "
"spectra in small studies, language or database bias, or chance"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>p < 0.05:</strong> The funnel plot is asymmetric - small studies "
"report systematically different accuracy from large ones. Publication bias "
"is one explanation; heterogeneity, a threshold effect, and a genuine "
"relation between study size and accuracy (smaller studies often use narrower "
"spectra) are others. The test cannot tell them apart, so a significant "
"result is a prompt to investigate, not a finding of publication bias"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>p ≥ 0.05:</strong> No statistically significant funnel-plot "
"asymmetry was detected. This does NOT indicate that publication bias is "
"unlikely: Deeks' test has low power and is unreliable with fewer than 10 "
"studies, so a non-significant result is uninformative when few studies are "
"pooled"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Patient population:</strong> Disease stage, demographics (e.g., "
"'early_stage', 'advanced', 'mixed')"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Patient Population:</strong> Disease stage, severity, demographics"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Point Scatter:</strong> Studies clustered tightly = consistent test "
"performance"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Pooled Estimate:</strong> Large triangle showing meta-analytic "
"summary"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Pooled Estimates:</strong> Sensitivity and specificity with "
"confidence intervals at the level chosen in Advanced Options"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Pooled Sensitivity:</strong> Proportion of diseased cases correctly "
"identified <ul> <li>Excellent sensitivity (≥90%): few diseased cases missed"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Pooled Specificity:</strong> Proportion of non-diseased cases "
"correctly identified <ul> <li>Excellent specificity (≥90%): few false alarms"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Positive Likelihood Ratio:</strong> %.2f - A positive test is %.1fx "
"more likely in disease than healthy"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Positive Likelihood Ratio:</strong> Not estimable with the current "
"data (specificity ≈ 100% or model unstable)."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Prediction region (dotted):</strong> where the accuracy of a FUTURE "
"study in a new setting is expected to fall. It includes between-study "
"heterogeneity and does NOT shrink with more studies."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Proportional-Hazards SROC Analysis:</strong> Holling model estimated "
"by adjusted profile maximum likelihood"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Proportional-hazards SROC modeling</strong> - Models the trade-off "
"between sensitivity and false-positive rate"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Publication Bias Assessment:</strong> Deeks' funnel plot asymmetry "
"test"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Publication Bias:</strong> Deeks' test results and visual assessment"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Publication bias</strong> - Checks for selective reporting"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Publication year:</strong> For temporal trend investigation"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>REML (Random Effects, Default):</strong> Estimates between-study "
"variance (τ²) to construct the SROC regression curve and prediction region. "
"Recommended for diagnostic meta-analysis because test thresholds and patient "
"populations inherently vary across studies."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>REML (Recommended):</strong> Default choice for most diagnostic "
"meta-analyses. Most robust for random effects modeling with good performance "
"across different scenarios."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Right Panel (Specificity):</strong> Proportion of healthy cases "
"correctly identified"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Row order:</strong> studies are sorted by their mean of sensitivity "
"and specificity, so each study keeps the same row in both panels and can be "
"read across. Within one panel the rows are therefore <em>not</em> ranked by "
"that panel's own metric"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Sample Size Range:</strong> %s - %s per study"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Scale:</strong> both panels run from 0 to 1; the tables report the "
"same quantities as percentages"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Screening Applications:</strong> Prioritize sensitivity; the level "
"needed depends on prevalence and on the cost of a missed case, not on a "
"fixed band"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Sensitivity:</strong> %.1f%% - The test correctly identifies %.0f "
"out of 1,000 patients with disease"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Sensitivity:</strong> %.1f%% - The test correctly identifies %.0f "
"out of 100 patients with disease"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Sensitivity:</strong> Mean %.1f%% (Range: %.1f%% - %.1f%%)"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Small Sample:</strong> Funnel plot unreliable with &lt;10 studies"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Small Studies Present:</strong> Range of precision levels represented"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Specificity:</strong> %.1f%% - The test correctly identifies %.0f "
"out of 1,000 healthy individuals"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Specificity:</strong> %.1f%% - The test correctly identifies %.0f "
"out of 100 healthy individuals"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Specificity:</strong> Mean %.1f%% (Range: %.1f%% - %.1f%%)"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>SROC Plot:</strong> Summary Receiver Operating Characteristic curve "
"showing the trade-off between sensitivity and specificity across all studies."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Step 1:</strong> Select your study identifier variable"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Step 2:</strong> Assign TP, FP, FN, TN count variables"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Step 3:</strong> Choose analysis options (bivariate recommended)"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Step 4:</strong> Review pooled estimates and heterogeneity"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Step 5:</strong> Interpret in your clinical context"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Studies corrected:</strong> %d of %d (%s)"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Study identifier:</strong> Unique name or ID for each study (e.g., "
"'Smith_2020', 'Study_1')"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Study Selection:</strong> Number of studies included and excluded"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Symmetric Funnel:</strong> Studies distributed evenly on both sides"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Technical Factors:</strong> Staining protocols, antibody sources, "
"automation"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Technical method:</strong> Staining protocol (e.g., 'automated', "
"'manual')"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>These bands are a descriptive convention, not a fitness-for-use "
"judgement.</strong> Whether an assay is adequate for screening, confirmation "
"or any other role depends on the prevalence in the population you intend to "
"test and on the clinical cost of a false negative versus a false positive - "
"none of which this analysis knows. Read the prediction interval alongside "
"the pooled pair before applying either to practice."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>These models answer related questions using different "
"parameterizations:</strong>"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Total Sample Size:</strong> %s participants"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>True negatives (TN):</strong> Number correctly identified as negative"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>True positives (TP):</strong> Number correctly identified as positive"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Upper Left Corner:</strong> Ideal performance (high sensitivity, low "
"false positive rate)"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Use this plot to:</strong> Compare the studies' operating points, "
"see the trade-off between sensitivity and specificity across them, and "
"assess consistency across study populations and settings. The curve "
"summarizes studies with unknown and differing positivity thresholds and "
"carries no threshold values, so it does not identify a cut-off for a "
"laboratory to adopt."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Use this when:</strong> Studies use the same diagnostic threshold"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Variable Performance:</strong> high heterogeneity indicates that "
"accuracy differed across the included populations and protocols"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Variance Components:</strong> Specialized approach for variance "
"component estimation, typically used in advanced methodological research."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Wide CIs:</strong> Small studies with less precise estimates"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Wide Spread:</strong> Points scattered = high heterogeneity "
"(investigate sources)"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>X-axis:</strong> False Positive Rate (1 - Specificity) - lower is "
"better"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>X-axis:</strong> Log Diagnostic Odds Ratio (effect size)"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Y-axis:</strong> 1/√(effective sample size), reversed so the most "
"precise studies sit at the top (Deeks' funnel plot)"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid "<strong>Y-axis:</strong> True Positive Rate (Sensitivity) - higher is better"
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Your negative LR of %.2f</strong> provides <em>minimal</em> evidence "
"AGAINST disease when test is negative."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Your negative LR of %.2f</strong> provides <em>moderate</em> "
"evidence AGAINST disease when test is negative."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Your negative LR of %.2f</strong> provides <em>strong</em> evidence "
"AGAINST disease when test is negative."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Your negative LR of %.2f</strong> provides <em>weak</em> evidence "
"AGAINST disease when test is negative."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Your pooled sensitivity of %.1f%%%s</strong> falls in the "
"<em>excellent</em> band on the conventional 90/80/70 scale."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Your pooled sensitivity of %.1f%%%s</strong> falls in the "
"<em>good</em> band on the conventional 90/80/70 scale."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Your pooled sensitivity of %.1f%%%s</strong> falls in the "
"<em>limited</em> band on the conventional 90/80/70 scale."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"<strong>Your pooled sensitivity of %.1f%%%s</strong> falls in the "
"<em>moderate</em> band on the conventional 90/80/70 scale."
msgstr ""

#: R/waterfall.b.R
msgid ""
"≤-100% change from baseline (simplified criterion, NOT full RECIST CR which "
"requires disappearance of ALL lesions including non-target)"
msgstr ""

#: R/waterfall.b.R
msgid ""
"≤-30% change from baseline (simplified criterion, NOT full RECIST PR which "
"requires target lesion sum calculation and no new lesions)"
msgstr ""

#: R/waterfall.b.R
