#: R/ihcheterogeneity.b.R
msgid ""
"Where every case differs by exactly the same amount, the precision of that "
"difference cannot be estimated: no p-value, effect size, CI or limits of "
"agreement are reported, and the difference is never ruled out. It is called "
"material only when every case lies beyond the margin in the same direction "
"and there are enough cases for a sign test at the adjusted level."
msgstr ""

#: R/diagnosticmeta.b.R
msgid ""
"Where more than a quarter of the studies have a zero cell, no verdict is "
"reported, as a precaution: the continuity correction the test needs can "
"produce asymmetry by itself (up to 42% of null meta-analyses at 80% "
"zero-cell studies in this module's simulations). The test always uses the "
"raw counts, so the Zero-Cell Correction option does not change this"
msgstr ""

#: R/ihcheterogeneity.b.R
msgid ""
"Where the slope was shown, the limits of agreement, which assume a "
"difference that does not change with the level, are too wide in the middle "
"of the range and too narrow at its ends."
msgstr ""

#: R/ihcheterogeneity.b.R
msgid ""
"Whether a difference changed with the level (proportional bias) was assessed "
"by regressing it on the mean of the two regions compared, with "
"heteroscedasticity-robust standard errors (Bland & Altman 1999); where the "
"slope differed from zero (p below 0.05 divided by the number of "
"comparisons), the difference was also judged at the 5th and 95th percentiles "
"of that level, and a difference that changed with the level was not "
"considered ruled out."
msgstr ""

#: R/ihcheterogeneity.b.R
msgid ""
"Whether a difference changed with the level (proportional bias) was assessed "
"by regressing it on the reference value, on the region value and on the mean "
"of the two, with heteroscedasticity-robust standard errors (Bland & Altman "
"1995, 1999); because measurement error alone tilts the first slope downwards "
"and the second upwards, a difference was considered to change with the level "
"only when all three slopes differed from zero in the same direction (p below "
"0.05 divided by the number of comparisons) (Krouwer 2008). Such a difference "
"was judged at the 5th and 95th percentiles of the mean of the two "
"measurements and was considered ruled out only if it lay within the margin "
"at both."
msgstr ""

#: R/ihcheterogeneity.b.R
msgid ""
"Whether a difference changes with the level was not assessed (too few cases "
"or distinct values)."
msgstr ""

